The libretro front end's RTG path: options, geometry and adm_get_rtg_state

The core side of RTG is now built; this is how it reaches the front end.

**The board.** puae_gfxcard_size fits an uaegfx board of that many megabytes, and
puae_gfxcard_type picks Zorro II or Zorro III. There is no "auto": every model preset leaves
gfxcard_size at 0, so an automatic setting could only ever mean "None", and the option exists
to turn on something no preset turns on. Both rows are written together - a gfxcard_size with
no gfxcard_type leaves rtgmem_type at 0, which is Zorro II, silently caps the board at 8 MB and
puts it in the wrong address space. A Zorro III board also turns 24-bit addressing off, exactly
as puae_z3mem_size already does for Z3 Fast RAM.

Neither is applied to changed_prefs while running, unlike the RAM options above them. The board
is an autoconfig device: it is enumerated once at reset, and moving it under a guest whose
Picasso96 has already found it would strand every pointer that guest holds.

Two traps here, both of which produced a board that silently did not exist.

**The rows are written by adm_machine_overrides_config(), not at the GET_VAR.** uae_config
reaches the machine from four of retro_create_config()'s five branches. The fifth - the one that
boots a .uae config file - reads that file row by row and then goes straight to
retro_config_kickstart(), so nothing in uae_config is applied at all. Peekaboo boots every
hard-disk, WHDLoad and CD machine through a generated launch.uae, which means a board written
into uae_config is fitted on a floppy or an empty boot and missing on every machine that has a
hard drive - that is, on every machine anyone would want RTG on.
adm_machine_overrides_config() runs after all five branches, which is exactly why the chipset
options already live there. It also puts cpu_24bit_addressing=false after the config file's own
rows rather than before them, which is where it needs to be.

**Each option is read into its own buffer, and neither is looked up from inside the other's
block.** A front end is free to answer GET_VARIABLE out of a single buffer it reuses - Peekaboo
does, one std::string - so a second lookup made while `var.value` from the first is still live
rewrites the string that pointer refers to. Doing it the obvious way wrote a row reading
`gfxcard_size=ZorroIII`, which cfgfile.c parsed as a 0 MB board.

**The picture.** Picasso96 renders into retro_bmp - the same buffer the chipset frame uses, and
only one of the two is live at a time (adisplays[0].picasso_on) - so nothing new has to be
allocated or presented. What does change is the size of the picture, and there the chipset
machinery has to be stood down rather than corrected:

  * update_audiovideo() returns early while an RTG screen is up. Everything in it reads BPLCON0,
    the DIW registers and the interlace state to work out the geometry, and those say nothing at
    all while the chipset display is switched off. The geometry is whatever Picasso96 asked for,
    with no crop. Leaving RTG sets the same update flags, so the chipset geometry is recomputed
    and re-announced even if it lands on the size it had before.
  * retro_get_aspect_ratio() returns width/height. An RTG mode has square pixels: none of the
    PAL/NTSC pixel aspect, the line doubling or the hires division applies.

The existing machinery does the rest - retro_update_av_info() already grows retrow_max/retroh_max
and issues SET_SYSTEM_AV_INFO when a frame gets bigger than anything seen before, which is
exactly what happens the first time a 1024x768 screen opens.

**The host.** adm_get_rtg_state() reports the board size, the screen the guest last opened and
whether RTG is the display right now. Peekaboo needs the last one for more than a status line:
every raster overlay it draws - copper, DMA, blitter, sprites - maps a beam position onto the
frame, and an RTG frame has no beam, so all of them are suppressed while this says RTG.

The depth it reports is the guest's, not the host's: a 256-colour Workbench reads 8 even though
the core is handing the front end 16- or 32-bit pixels.

gfxmem_bank is exported too, so the board's VRAM shows in Peekaboo's Memory Map like every other
bank - which is also how the tests prove the board was really fitted.

Applies to libretro-uae 2b0ed42fe565fb997a0627aaa8f44e0948b527f8 in the order given by
patches/series.txt.

